// CF-2: a discard inside a DYNAMIC loop must refuse, and this is a
// DIVERGENCE from the reference rather than a shared refusal.
//
// The reference compiles this with real hardware loop instructions -
// LOOPH / BRKH / ENDLOOP around the comparison and the kill.  Our general
// path refuses the back-edge (CF-1a) and the default path has no shape
// for it.  The fixture exists so that refusal is SEEN to fire on a
// discard-carrying loop, not merely believed.
//
// The loop bound must be dynamic.  A constant-trip loop is not a
// substitute: our frontend fully unrolls one - measured, four discards
// and four $kill parameters - so it never produces a back-edge and the
// refusal would never be reached.
void main(float4 c : TEXCOORD0, uniform float u_n, out float4 o : COLOR)
{
    float s = 0.0;
    for (float i = 0.0; i < u_n; i += 1.0) {
        s += c.x;
        if (s > 2.0) discard;
    }
    o = float4(s, 0, 0, 1);
}
